WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.847
Reference counting solves some ownership
problems,

2
00:00:02.927 --> 00:00:06.109
but it can introduce lifecycle problems
when strong owners form cycles.

3
00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:09.580
If two values strongly own each other, the
strong counts may never reach zero.

4
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.433
Safe Rust still protects memory safety,

5
00:00:11.513 --> 00:00:14.122
but the values may not be cleaned up when
the design expects.

6
00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:15.923
A leak is not automatically an exploit,

7
00:00:16.003 --> 00:00:18.142
and safe Rust prevents many dangerous
outcomes,

8
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:19.943
but leaks still matter in real systems.

9
00:00:20.023 --> 00:00:23.466
Long-running services, background workers,
desktop applications, caches,

10
00:00:23.546 --> 00:00:26.624
and agents can suffer resource growth,
stale state, delayed cleanup,

11
00:00:26.704 --> 00:00:29.833
and operational instability when lifecycle
behavior is not reviewed.

12
00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:33.252
`Weak<T>` models a non-owning relationship
to a reference-counted value.

13
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.366
A weak reference does not keep the value
alive.

14
00:00:35.446 --> 00:00:39.045
Code must attempt to upgrade the weak
reference and handle the case where the value

15
00:00:39.125 --> 00:00:40.273
has already been dropped.

16
00:00:40.353 --> 00:00:43.170
That checked access makes non-owning
relationships explicit.

17
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:46.223
This is useful for parent-child
relationships, back-references,

18
00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:49.224
observer lists, caches, registries, and
graph-like structures.

19
00:00:49.304 --> 00:00:52.904
A child may need to know about its parent
without keeping the parent alive forever.

20
00:00:52.984 --> 00:00:56.374
A cache may want to remember a value if it
still exists but not force it to remain

21
00:00:56.454 --> 00:00:56.976
allocated.

22
00:00:57.056 --> 00:01:00.081
The review question is whether each
relationship should own, share,

23
00:01:00.161 --> 00:01:00.996
or merely observe.

24
00:01:01.076 --> 00:01:04.154
Strong references should represent real
ownership responsibility.

25
00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:07.103
Weak references should represent optional,
non-owning access.

26
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:10.417
When that distinction is clear, cleanup
behavior becomes easier to test,

27
00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:11.540
maintain, and explain.
